panvk/kmod: abandoned JM work ends as failed, and every JM wait for the queue's work has a time limit

Patch 0006 gives the job manager's waits for idle and for the device's end a time limit of 10 s from
the call, and then abandons the work still in flight: it drops the callbacks. A dropped callback
leaves the work's sync objects SUBMITTED for ever, the other waits for that work have no limit
(Zink's vkWaitSemaphores with UINT64_MAX; pan_kmod_kbase_wait_seq for a command buffer used again,
for the BC refresh ring and for an emulated BC image; a submission's wait for free atoms, for room
in open_seq and for the work before it), and a limit from the call takes a long queue of valid work
for a lost device.

This patch:
- pan_kmod_kbase_abandon() ends the submissions still in flight as failed: it runs each callback
  once, with ok false (so the sync objects are signaled; with patch 0008 the callback reads nothing
  that the app can free once the device is lost), and then the submissions are no longer open, so
  every wait for them returns. The event thread still frees their atoms if they report later, but
  it runs no callback of them and does not end their open seq: abandon ends it after the callback,
  so a blocking submitter, whose callback writes to its stack, waits until then. abandon counts its
  own callbacks as running and returns once no callback runs, so a second call returns only after
  the callbacks of the first. From the first call on, nothing is submitted (submit_async returns 0).
- Every wait of panvk for submitted work on the job manager has the limit (panvk_kbase_wait_seq):
  vkQueueWaitIdle and the queue's destroy, and the waits for a command buffer used again, for the
  BC refresh ring's slot and for an emulated BC image's last refresh. When the work sends no event
  for 10 s (1 s on a device that is lost already: vkQueueWaitIdle then waits too, and abandons),
  the device is lost (first, so that the callbacks read no command buffer), panvk logs "panvk: the
  queue's work did not report in N s; it ends as failed", and the work is abandoned.
- A submission's waits in submit_async (for free atoms and room in open_seq, and for the work
  before it when it has nothing to run) end at the same limit, or when the queue is abandoned:
  then it is not sent (0), and the kmod logs "kbase: the queue's work sent no event in 10 s".
  The caller then loses the device and abandons the work, as it does when the kernel refuses a
  submission or a part of a large submission.
- The limits count from the last event of the work, not from the call
  (pan_kmod_kbase_wait_seq_timeout takes a time without an event; the event thread records when it
  reads events), so a long queue of valid work is not a lost device.
- The log names the oldest open submission (the table is not in order) and says "has not reported
  yet" for each atom still in flight, once.

